5
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Bird migration has been studied over many centuries through a variety of observations,

6
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:45,000
but until relatively recently where birds went to in the winter was considered something of a mystery.

7
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,000
The lack of modern science and technology led to many theories that we now recognise as

8
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:59,000
error-filled and even somewhat amusing. Take hibernation theory, for example.

9
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:10,000
2,000 years ago it was commonly believed that when birds left an area they went underwater to hibernate in the seas and oceans.

10
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Another theory for the regular appearance and disappearance of birds is the lack of

11
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:23,000
another theory for the regular appearance and disappearance of birds was that they spent

12
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
winter hidden in mud till the weather changed and food became abundant again.

13
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
The theory that some birds hibernate persisted until experiments were done on caged birds in the 1940s,

14
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,000
which demonstrated that birds have no hibernation instinct.

15
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:50,000
One of the earliest naturalists and philosophers from ancient Greece was Aristotle,

16
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:59,000
who was the first writer to discuss the disappearance and reappearance of some bird species at certain times of year.

17
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:06,000
He developed the theory of transmutation, the seasonal change of one species into another

18
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:16,000
by observing redstarts and robins. He observed that in the autumn small birds called redstarts began to lose their feathers,

19
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:25,000
which convinced Aristotle that they changed into robins for the winter and back into redstarts in the summer.

20
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:32,000
These assumptions are understandable given that this pair of species are similar in shape

21
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:40,000
but are a classic example of an incorrect interpretation based on correct observations.

22
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:48,000
The most bizarre theory was put forward by an English amateur scientist, Charles Morton, in the 17th century.

23
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:57,000
He wrote a surprisingly well regarded paper claiming that birds migrate to the moon and back every year.

24
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:08,000
He came to this conclusion as the only logical explanation for the total disappearance of some species.

25
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:19,000
One of the key moments in the development of migration theory came in 1822, when a white stalk was shot in Germany.

26
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:28,000
This particular stalk made history because of the long spear in its neck, which incredibly had not killed it.

27
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:34,000
Everyone immediately realised this spear was definitely not European.

28
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:39,000
It turned out to be a spear from a tribe in Central Africa.

29
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:51,000
This was a truly defining moment in the history of ornithology because it was the first evidence that stalks spend their winters in sub-Saharan Africa.

30
00:04:51,000 --> 00:05:00,000
You can still see the arrow stalk in the zoological collection of the University of Rostock in Germany.

31
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:08,000
People gradually became aware that European birds moved south in autumn and north in summer,

32
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:16,000
but didn't know much about it until the practice of catching birds and putting rings on their legs became established.

33
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Before this, very little information was available about the actual destinations of particular species and how they travelled there.

34
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:35,000
People speculated that larger birds provided a kind of taxi service for smaller birds by carrying them on their backs.

35
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:45,000
This idea came about because it seemed impossible that small birds weighing only a few grams could fly over vast oceans.

36
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:55,000
This idea was supported by observations of bird behaviour, such as the harassment of larger birds by smaller birds.

37
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:05,000
The development of bird ringing by a Danish schoolteacher, Hans Christian Cornelius Mortensen, made many discoveries possible.

38
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:11,000
This is still common practice today and relies upon what is known as recovery.

39
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:18,000
This is when ringed birds are found dead in the place they have migrated to and identified.

40
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Huge amounts of data were gathered in the early part of the 20th century, and for the first time in history, people understood where birds actually went to in winter.

41
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:39,000
In 1931, an atlas was published showing where the most common species of European birds migrated to.

42
